#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084331} :activity/id #uuid "4c50d575-0f8a-592f-8bee-9a211447ec98" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847 :topic/id #uuid "6b5cc7c5-f3ba-5a78-aceb-e5c263dbe224" :topic/math-academy-id 765} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084412 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \n}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "43cdd368-7207-5d18-8f22-5e384ebbc118" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2457 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76d3dfad-1625-5612-88e9-44f6df730e85" :step/math-academy-id 453556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084332}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083364 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082956 :question/id #uuid "58cfd10d-afd2-5678-a38a-e0fbc0221076" :question/math-academy-id "e-3272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a622bad-6820-5e34-a347-890709103bc0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049915}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527959}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 45 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "2f3823b3-f0cb-5b53-ab96-dc48e9c09690" :step/math-academy-id 453557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084413 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle whose shortest leg has a length equal to $1,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/67/679ce670484e57ba699030de4cf6d9ba52a5d6910d22b4beb2ad5d4e8ac4e0e0.png)\n\nNow, we can calculate some trigonometric ratios without a calculator. Let's start with the $30^\\circ$-angle. By the definition of sine, cosine, and tangent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the $60^\\circ$-angle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87688da-9a24-5f3e-9160-de9562217a11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2460 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Ratios for 30-60-90 Triangles"} :step/id #uuid "60a44c52-c126-53ea-b949-1b44b8614b1c" :step/math-academy-id 453561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083365 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082957 :question/id #uuid "b593adba-7766-5cce-b607-ae3f2ad24970" :question/math-academy-id "e-3275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "462eeb28-d618-5c29-8568-186d63645f99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551341}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 30 and 60 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "bf2f2513-8031-5383-8527-049ce8fb6c88" :step/math-academy-id 453562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084414 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d78c0611-a7ea-5bc0-830b-fb04311281da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2461 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the Zero Degree Angle"} :step/id #uuid "34c6a603-c08d-5f28-95f6-03d5cf07c17e" :step/math-academy-id 453566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082958 :question/id #uuid "0959222f-542e-5cea-b10a-fa3872c93571" :question/math-academy-id "e-3278"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59c7be47-27c1-5300-8fd9-0e7d39e977d9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551280}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 0 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "efdf3091-c5e4-5290-a97f-f9040128b097" :step/math-academy-id 453567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084415 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A,$ the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ.$ Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 90^\\circ,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=\\frac{AC}{0}=\\text{undefined.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." :tutorial/id #uuid "c602245b-ade3-5bc6-8703-b73dd0425cba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2462 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the 90 Degree Angle"} :step/id #uuid "d2d14fdc-c970-5852-b625-d0487c11289e" :step/math-academy-id 453570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082959 :question/id #uuid "bac66691-92d3-56d4-84a1-7502ba095e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-3281"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a330418-1f66-5dfa-9543-8c0d5ec6e8f2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551324}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions With 90 Degree Angles"} :step/id #uuid "4c9fa83a-1c50-5837-8af1-69e0fc1506ac" :step/math-academy-id 453571}] :activity/title "Special Trigonometric Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083454}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551404} :activity/id #uuid "a10d6d36-1f91-5420-9d67-cff0214d8f55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847 :topic/id #uuid "6b5cc7c5-f3ba-5a78-aceb-e5c263dbe224" :topic/math-academy-id 765} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0808b740-b9a6-53e0-b0ca-072927115f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550390} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f936aef-f06f-5e4a-ab28-0116084a830b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88c09c1c-1ce0-5b01-83e5-a18e9189dbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-244571" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles of ${60}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d08ae5a-3900-5a4e-830c-45eae619438c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551380 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499727a0-3e5b-5bde-ab2e-9a316650fcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550541} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc67d17-be4b-5361-a70d-ecc1ca22279a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550556} {:answer/id #uuid "860778fd-bed5-5bee-ad73-89d756a4e533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550706} {:answer/id #uuid "e6954fa0-b6f7-5d60-9121-9e71a1f8c744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550994} {:answer/id #uuid "e80bfc0b-e9a2-5c8d-b1f4-256775d3038c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550556} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce869806-ba63-5a18-8a07-5e15d5ad7298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "602ff191-9fc8-5da7-a56d-e21a1e6c8aee" :question/math-academy-id "q-28935" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${45}^{∘},$ ${30}^{∘}$ and ${0}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "306a8d26-1ef7-5b4b-a318-0b8e137317b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae499bb-bf8f-5fc4-aec9-2dc9bdb9a4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550590} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea00391-81e6-5aa5-92c3-aab074a102d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550683} {:answer/id #uuid "7fee911c-477b-55c3-9770-f92077500b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550691} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2f2ca5-e104-5920-9146-0e49e1295162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550769} {:answer/id #uuid "a898a109-7268-53b6-8036-8d9cf20ccf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550590} :answer-field/id #uuid "edfb1d06-ff84-5a5e-b3ca-4378a3fc65ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da211e88-d789-5f6a-b87d-2366e18b07f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-5991" :question/problem "$1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angle ${30}^{∘}$ is special, and its sine has the value\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b7dbe22-dc0d-54cb-b4df-f39f966687ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc9fa93-f40e-5310-8f1c-3a09ec82f709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550722} :answer-field/id #uuid "2955e3b5-359a-52c1-ba32-374efd6a1771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aec99370-575e-52b4-9e36-bb43c957f2bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-266852" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles of ${0}^{∘},$ ${45}^{∘},$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1+0-1 & = \\\\ 0. & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d66e22f-32f8-524d-9151-c3676a742fc5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420931a7-bfe9-57fd-be28-96ee8c3e7a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550522} {:answer/id #uuid "52b2408a-7f36-5c00-b12f-5d5d12445119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550564} {:answer/id #uuid "7676748a-b3e1-574b-8bbc-7af2c704efdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550665} {:answer/id #uuid "847735f3-85e7-556f-ab13-996c4f671091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550702} {:answer/id #uuid "8a774edc-0a83-5eff-b7f3-8dd8b4849f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550718} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a43897-150e-534f-a498-9f892cf924c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f16c2b9-1c8b-50a4-93ab-bbcc2578659f" :question/math-academy-id "q-5994" :question/problem "$3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${30}^{∘}$ and ${45}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have the  values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}$ simply means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ 3{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})}^{2}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ 3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}-1 & = \\\\ 3(\\frac{3}{9})-1 & = \\\\ 1-1 & = \\\\ 0 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9adf5944-13ef-501f-b0c3-0fc0d68be6fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551380}}] :activity/title "Special Trigonometric Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550357}]}
